mlterm-fb-dumper

* What

  Test/debug tool for the fb-embed fork. Drives an embed-mode mlterm
  with a stream of VT bytes (from stdin or a file), renders one frame,
  and writes the resulting framebuffer as a PPM image.

  Built only when configure was run with --enable-fb-embed (the rest
  of the time it's not useful — there's no embed API to call).

* Usage

  mlterm-fb-dumper [-c COLS] [-r ROWS] [-o OUT.ppm] [-f INPUT.vt]

    -c COLS    grid columns       (default 80)
    -r ROWS    grid rows          (default 24)
    -o PATH    output PPM path    (default stdout)
    -f PATH    input script path  (default stdin)

  Without -f, reads VT bytes from stdin until EOF. Without -o, writes
  PPM to stdout — pipe through ImageMagick or feh for live preview:

    printf '\033[1;36mhello\r\n' | mlterm-fb-dumper | display -

* Why PPM and not PNG

  PPM (P6 raw RGB) has zero deps and a trivial header. cmp(1) gives
  byte-exact diffs; standard image viewers all display it; converting
  to PNG is one ImageMagick or netpbm call away. Adding libpng would
  drag a dep into a tool that's only useful in a development context.

* Use as a regression suite

  Capture a known-good rendering once, commit it, then regress against
  it on every refactor:

    # capture once
    cat tests/inputs/colors.vt | mlterm-fb-dumper > tests/golden/colors.ppm

    # later, after refactoring
    cat tests/inputs/colors.vt | mlterm-fb-dumper > /tmp/colors.ppm
    cmp tests/golden/colors.ppm /tmp/colors.ppm

  Sixel and ReGIS payloads pass through the parser straight to the
  renderer, so a golden capture exercises the full pipeline including
  graphics — the test covers what jexer never could.
